          "verdict": "plausible",
          "derivation": "Avestan taoš- / tuš- 'to be empty, loose' is well attested (Bartholomae col. 624). Its inchoative present tusa- appears in tusən 'they lose composure, grow weak' (V. 3.32), and its causative taošaya- means 'let loose, let fall' (Yt. 10.48). All of these continue PIE *tews- / *tus-sḱé-. 'tussya' is not an attested Avestan word. It mirrors the reconstruction *tus-sk-io- (Skt. tucchya-) behind Middle Persian tuhīg, Proto-Iranian *tušyá-. The label 'Old Persian' is also unsupported, since this root is not attested in the Old Persian inscriptions. The Avestan forms are confirmed; the OP label and 'tussya' as an attested form are not.",
